0" fill="currentColor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="icon"><path d="M15.498 8.50159C16.3254 8.50159 16.9959 9.17228 16.9961 9.99963C16.9961 10.8271 16.3256 11.4987 15.498 11.4987C14.6705 11.4987 14 10.8271 14 9.99963C14.0002 9.17228 14.6706 8.50159 15.498 8.50159Z"></path><path d="M4.49805 8.50159C5.32544 8.50159 5.99689 9.17228 5.99707 9.99963C5.99707 10.8271 5.32555 11.4987 4.49805 11.4987C3.67069 11.4985 3 10.827 3 9.99963C3.00018 9.17239 3.6708 8.50176 4.49805 8.50159Z"></path><path d="M10.0003 8.50159C10.8276 8.50176 11.4982 9.17239 11.4984 9.99963C11.4984 10.827 10.8277 11.4985 10.0003 11.4987C9.17283 11.4987 8.50131 10.8271 8.50131 9.99963C8.50149 9.17228 9.17294 8.50159 10.0003 8.50159Z"></path></svg></button></div></div></div></header><div class="relative basis-auto flex-col -mb-(--composer-overlap-px) [--composer-overlap-px:28px] grow flex overflow-hidden" style="--composer-height: 56px;"><div class="relative h-full" style="--pip-widget-height: 0px;"><div class="flex h-full flex-col overflow-y-auto thread-xl:pt-(--header-height) [scrollbar-gutter:stable_both-edges]"><div aria-hidden="true" data-edge="true" class="pointer-events-none h-px w-px"></div><div class="flex flex-col text-sm thread-xl:pt-header-height keyboard-open:pb-[calc(var(--composer-height,100px)+var(--screen-keyboard-height,0))] pb-25"><article class="text-token-text-primary w-full focus:outline-none scroll-mt-(--header-height)" tabindex="-1" dir="auto" data-turn-id="bbb215dc-9b2d-40ee-9d34-8eed409df01e" data-testid="conversation-turn-1" data-scroll-anchor="false" data-turn="user"><h5 class="sr-only">You said:</h5><div class="text-base my-auto mx-auto pt-3 [--thread-content-margin:--spacing(4)] thread-sm:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(6)] thread-lg:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(16)] px-(--thread-content-margin)"><div class="[--thread-content-max-width:40rem] thread-lg:[--thread-content-max-width:48rem] mx-auto max-w-(--thread-content-max-width) flex-1 group/turn-messages focus-visible:outline-hidden relative flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col" tabindex="-1"><div class="flex max-w-full flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="user" data-message-id="bbb215dc-9b2d-40ee-9d34-8eed409df01e" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden items-end rtl:items-start"><div class="user-message-bubble-color relative rounded-[18px] px-4 py-1.5 data-[multiline]:py-3 max-w-[var(--user-chat-width,70%)]" data-multiline=""><div class="whitespace-pre-wrap">Raymond Arnold writes: Something I like about "If Anyone Builds It, Everyone Dies", is it the way it lays out disjunctive arguments.

Like: “We think ultimately, a naively developed superintelligence would want to kill everyone, for reasons A, B, C and D. Maybe you don’t buy reasons B, C and D. But that still leaves you with A, and here’s are argument that even if Reason A wouldn’t literally kill everyone, the expected outcome is still something horrifying.”

(An example of that was: For “might the AI keep us as pets?”, the book answers (paraphrased) “We don’t think so. But, even if they did… note that, while humans keep dogs as pets, we don’t keep wolves as pets. Look at the transform from wolf to dog. An AI might keep us as pets, but, if that’s your hope, imagine the transform from Wolves-to-Dogs and equivalent transforms on humans.”)

A similar thing I like in the AI Takeoff story in part 2 is that, instead of saying "and then x happens, and then y, and then z" (which invites the criticism of "but why did you pick x, y and z?")...

...instead, at each point, there are like "here are like 5 things the AI might do next. You might think some of them are unrealistic. Here's why we don't think it's unrealistic. But, assume it IS unrealistic, and none of those 5 things work, here's *another* few things it could do."

(Followed by "and, probably, the AI doesn't pick one of those actions. It does all of them, and it finds out which one works best, and then doubles down on that strategy.")

A criticism I’d make of Realtime Arguing Eliezer is that he often argues in a forceful way, rejecting bids to talk outside his frame. I did NOT feel this way while reading the book. I felt like the authors were arguing a thing they believe strongly, but arguments were presented in a way that felt easier to take in and think about. The book IMO does a pretty good job of taking the Yudkowskian worldview and sanding off some of the rough edges.

Overall, I would describe the book as "quite good."

It’s not a *great* book, IMO – it’s not Eliezer’s best work. But, it’s quite solid, it’s working under more difficult constraints than Eliezer’s best work, and I think the subject matter carries enough weight that even though the quality is merely Quite Good, it has a decent shot of being a well known, important piece of the zeitgeist that changes the discussion landscape.

The content is mostly not new if you've been thoroughly following Yudkowsky. But, there is a LOT of Yudkowsky out there. And some of the concepts in the book were previously only really described in very long meandering dialogues. The book is a pretty streamlined version that touches on each of the main arguments. (There's also online resources that delve deeper into various arguments and edge cases)

I recommend reading the book on it's own terms if you've only gotten piecemeal bits of the arguments for "no actually everyone will probably die – it's not 100% certain but it's the overwhelmingly likely thing you should expect." The book + website are the best way to ingest the MIRI worldview about AI, IMO.

Meanwhile, I do think it matters a lot that the book get to snowball into public consciousness, so if you're considering getting it, please buy it this week (before the 20th)</div></div></div></div></div><div class="flex justify-end"><div class="touch:-me-2 touch:-ms-3.5 -ms-2.5 -me-1 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-y-4 p-1 select-none focus-within:transition-none hover:transition-none duration-300 group-hover/turn-messages:delay-300 pointer-events-none opacity-0 motion-safe:transition-opacity group-hover/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-hover/turn-messages:opacity-100 group-focus-within/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-focus-within/turn-messages:opacity-100 has-data-[state=open]:pointer-events-auto has-data-[state=open]:opacity-100" style="mask-position: 0% 0%;"><button class="text-token-text-secondary hover:bg-token-bg-secondary rounded-lg" aria-label="Copy" aria-pressed="false" data-testid="copy-turn-action-button" data-state="closed"><span class="flex items-center justify-center touch:w-10 h-8 w-8"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="icon"><path d="M12.668 10.667C12.668 9.95614 12.668 9.46258 12.6367 9.0791C12.6137 8.79732 12.5758 8.60761 12.5244 8.46387L12.4688 8.33399C12.3148 8.03193 12.0803 7.77885 11.793 7.60254L11.666 7.53125C11.508 7.45087 11.2963 7.39395 10.9209 7.36328C10.5374 7.33197 10.0439 7.33203 9.33301 7.33203H6.5C5.78896 7.33203 5.29563 7.33195 4.91211 7.36328C4.63016 7.38632 4.44065 7.42413 4.29688 7.47559L4.16699 7.53125C3.86488 7.68518 3.61186 7.9196 3.43555 8.20703L3.36524 8.33399C3.28478 8.49198 3.22795 8.70352 3.19727 9.0791C3.16595 9.46259 3.16504 9.95611 3.16504 10.667V13.5C3.16504 14.211 3.16593 14.7044 3.19727 15.0879C3.22797 15.4636 3.28473 15.675 3.36524 15.833L3.43555 15.959C3.61186 16.2466 3.86474 16.4807 4.16699 16.6348L4.29688 16.6914C4.44063 16.7428 4.63025 16.7797 4.91211 16.8027C5.29563 16.8341 5.78896 16.835 6.5 16.835H9.33301C10.0439 16.835