.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-latex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>*{Appendix B: Entropy, Attack Surface, and Disjunctive Failure}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\addcontentsline</span></span><span>{toc}{section}{Appendix B: Entropy, Attack Surface, and Disjunctive Failure}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>*{Informal statement}
Entropy enlarges the accessible microstate space and hence the number of distinct macroscopic channels that can lead to irreversible collapse. In RSVP language, scalar potentials </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> define basins and barriers, vector flows </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span> v</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> explore those basins, and entropy </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> counts compatible microstates. Because the measure of ``safe'' microstates is typically small relative to the measure of ``dangerous'' microstates, the combinatorial count of doom pathways vastly exceeds the count of survival pathways.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>*{Formal model}
Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> denote the system microstate space with measure </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. Partition into absorbing regions:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span> = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{safe}} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cup</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{doom}},</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\qquad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{safe}}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cap</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{doom}}=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varnothing</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
Define effective macro-entropies </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{safe}}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{doom}}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> so that, up to normalization,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{safe}})</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\propto</span></span><span> e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{safe}}},</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\qquad</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{doom}})</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\propto</span></span><span> e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{doom}}}.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

The relative measure of safe to doom volume is therefore
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{safe}})}{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{doom}})} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sim</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\exp</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>(S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{safe}}-S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{doom}}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
A modest entropy gap </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{doom}}-S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{safe}})&gt;0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> yields an exponentially larger space of doom microstates.

If the system dynamics (driven by </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span> v</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and subject to stochastic perturbations) sample microstates with stationary density </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rho</span></span><span>(x)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, then, to leading order, the probability of eventual doom is approximated by the relative measure
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
P(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{doom}) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\approx</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{doom}})}{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span>)} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\approx</span></span><span> 1 - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{safe}})}{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span>)}.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>*{Flux and first-passage considerations}
For stochastic exploration (e.g., Langevin dynamics or other noisy optimizers), the hazard rate of entering </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{doom}}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is governed by the probability flux across the safe–doom boundary </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{safe}}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
J</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span>} = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\int</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{safe}}} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span> j</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span> n</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> dA,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span> j</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is the probability current and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span> n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> the outward normal. When </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{safe}}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is high-dimensional and contains many low-barrier channels, the aggregate flux </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>J</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span>}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> can be large even if each individual channel contributes little. Mean first passage times into </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{doom}}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> then become short on practical timescales.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>*{Implication for defensive design}
Listing defensive options corresponds to identifying low-dimensional subsets of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{safe}}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. To materially reduce </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>P(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{doom})</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> one must either:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{enumerate}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> reduce </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{doom}})</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> (shrink the entropy/measure of dangerous regions), or
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> increase barrier heights across many independent directions (raise the effective energy barriers between safe and doom regions), thereby suppressing aggregate flux.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{enumerate}
Because entropy multiplies attack surfaces combinatorially, defensive strategy must focus on measure-level changes, not merely a large but finite catalog of countermeasures.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
<hr data-start="6946" data-end="6949">
<h1 data-start="6951" data-end="7031">4 — Small extensions / modeling suggestions (if you want them in the appendix)</h1>
<ul data-start="7032" data-end="7643">
<li data-start="7032" data-end="7200">
<p data-start="7034" data-end="7200">Replace the proportionality <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>S</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{S}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8413em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal">e</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.8413em;"><span style="top: -3.063em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight" style="margin-right: 0.05764em;">S</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> with a mesh-dependent count if you want discrete microstate accounting (useful for computational lattice simulations of RSVP).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="7201" data-end="7475">
<p data-start="7203" data-end="7475">Model exploration as a Markov chain on coarse states; then the absorption probability equals 1 minus the probability mass on transient states in the long-time limit — this makes the “many doom paths” idea explicit in terms of transition graphs with many absorbing nodes.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="7476" data-end="7643">
<p data-start="7478" data-end="7643">For dynamical escape-rate analysis, include a Kramers-type expression for escape rate across a high-dimensional saddle to show how rate scales with barrier geometry.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="7645" data-end="7648">
<p data-start="7650" data-end="8122">If you want, I can (now, immediately) expand any of the above into a longer appendix: e.g., (A) derive a mean-first-passage estimate for a toy RSVP lattice, (B) write the Markov chain / absorbing state formalism with an explicit example where safe manifold volume is <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo>≪</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ll</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.5782em; vertical-align: -0.0391em;"></span><span class="mrel">≪</span></span></span></span> doom volume, or (C) convert the short appendix into a longer discussion with figures (LaTeX + TikZ) — though you’ve said you prefer to avoid diagrams, so I’ll skip graphics unless you request them.</p>
<p data-start="8124" data-end="8209" data-is-last-node="" data-is-only-node="">Which of those (A/B/C) would you like me to add straight into the LaTeX appendix now?</p></div></div></div></div><div class="flex min-h-[46px] justify-start"><div class="touch:-me-2 touch:-ms-3.5 -ms-2.5 -me-1 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-y-4 p-1 select-none touch:w-[calc(100%+--spacing(3.5))] -mt-1 w-[calc(100%+--spacing(2.5))] duration-[1.5s] focus-within:transition-none hover:transition-none pointer-events-none [mask-image:linear-gradient(to_right,black_33%,transparent_66%)] [mask-size:300%_100%] [mask-position:100%_0%] motion-safe:transition-[mask-position] group-hover/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-hover/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] group-focus-within/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-focus-within/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] has-data-[state=open]:pointer-events-auto has-data-[state=open]:[mask-position:0_0]" style="mask-position: 0% 0%;"><button class="text-token-text-secondary hover:bg-token-bg-secondary rounded-lg" aria-label="Copy" aria-pressed="false" data-testid="copy-turn-action-button" data-state="closed"><span class="flex items-center justify-center touch:w-10 h-8 w-8"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="icon"><path d="M12.668 10.667C12.668 9.95614 12.668 9.46258 12.6367 9.0791C12.6137 8.79732 12.5758 8.60761 12.5244 8.46387L12.4688 8.33399C12.3148 8.03193 12.0803 7.77885 11.793 7.60254L11.666 7.53125C11.508 7.45087 11.2963 7.39395 10.9209 7.36328C10.5374 7.33197 10.0439 7.33203 9.33301 7.33203H6.5C5.78896 7.33203 5.29563 7.33195 4.91211 7.36328C4.63016 7.38632 4.44065 7.42413 4.29688 7.47559L4.16699 7.53125C3.86488 7.68518 3.61186 7.9196 3.43555 8.20703L3.36524 8.33399C3.28478 8.49198 3.22795 8.70352 3.19727 9.0791C3.16595 9.46259 3.16504 9.95611 3.16504 10.667V13.5C3.16504 14.211 3.16593 14.7044 3.19727 15.0879C3.22797 15.4636 3.28473 15.675 3.36524 15.833L3.43555 15.959C3