01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-latex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Entropy as Attack Surface: Worked Formalisms}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{A. Mean First-Passage on a Toy RSVP Lattice}
Consider a coarse RSVP lattice with microstates </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>x</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subset</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{Z}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>d</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and a ``safe'' region </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{safe}}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subset</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> whose boundary is </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{safe}}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. Let the dynamics be a discrete-time Markov process with local updates </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>x</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mapsto</span></span><span> x+</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> are nearest-neighbor moves biased by (minus) the gradient of a coarse scalar potential </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and perturbed by noise of effective temperature </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varepsilon</span></span><span>&gt;0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{P}(x</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> x+</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\delta</span></span><span>) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\propto</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\exp</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\left</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span>-</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{1}{2</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varepsilon</span></span><span>}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>(x+</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\delta</span></span><span>)-</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>(x)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\right</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span>,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1=1.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> be the first-passage time from an interior </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{safe}}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> to </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{safe}}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. For a convex safe region of linear size </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> with weak drift toward the boundary and isotropic noise, the classic random-walk estimate yields
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{E}[</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>]</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\asymp</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2}{D</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{eff}}},</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\qquad</span></span><span>
D</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{eff}} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sim</span></span><span> c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>d</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varepsilon</span></span><span>,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>d</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> depends only on dimension and step geometry. Thus, even when </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is locally flat, </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{E}[</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>]</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> shrinks with rising noise </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varepsilon</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> (more exploration) and with shrinking </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> (thinner safety margins).

Now suppose there are </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> disjoint exit channels on </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{safe}}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> (``attack ports''), each associated to a narrow saddle with barrier height </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and local curvatures encoded by Hessians </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> (at the attractor in </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{safe}}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>) and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{s,i}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> (at the </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>th saddle). In the small-noise limit, the Kramers–Langer escape rate from the attractor region is, to leading order,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
k</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\approx</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{s,i}}{2</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span>}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sqrt</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\det</span></span><span> H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a}{|</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\det</span></span><span> H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{s,i}|}}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\exp</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\left</span></span><span>(-</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i}{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varepsilon</span></span><span>}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\right</span></span><span>),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
with </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{s,i}&gt;0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> the magnitude of the unique unstable eigenvalue at saddle </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. Since exits are in parallel, the total hazard is additive:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
k</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{tot}} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\approx</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{i=1}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{M} k</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
Hence, even if each </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>k</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is small, a large channel count </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>---the entropic multiplicity of ways to fail---can dominate </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>k</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{tot}}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and drive </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{E}[</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>]</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\approx</span></span><span> 1/k</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{tot}}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> to practically small values. Defensive design must therefore </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{both} (i) raise </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{all} </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> meaningfully and (ii) reduce </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> (seal channels), or </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>k</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{tot}}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> remains large.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Corollary (Scaling).}
If </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and local prefactors are comparable (</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\approx</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>), then
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
k</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{tot}} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\approx</span></span><span> M</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{-</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>/</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varepsilon</span></span><span>},</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\qquad</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{E}[</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>] </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\approx</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>/</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varepsilon</span></span><span>}}{M</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span>}.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
