strong data-start="19" data-end="50">two 1-page lesson templates</strong> you can drop into an appendix or hand to teachers. They’re written in a compact, classroom-ready style (objectives, activity, wrap-up), with the framing you suggested: calculus and logic are <em data-start="242" data-end="248">easy</em> and intuitive, not mysterious.</p>
<hr data-start="283" data-end="286">
<h1 data-start="288" data-end="366">Lesson Template 1: Calculus is Easy — Linear Approximation &amp; Error Estimates</h1>
<p data-start="368" data-end="551"><strong data-start="368" data-end="414">Objective (10s elevator pitch to students)</strong><br data-start="414" data-end="417">
Slopes tell us how things change. With one slope you can guess nearby values. That’s calculus: using slopes to make quick predictions.</p>
<p data-start="553" data-end="767"><strong data-start="553" data-end="572">Warm-up (5 min)</strong><br data-start="572" data-end="575">
Ask: “If a car is at 50 mph now, what speed do you expect 10 seconds later if it accelerates 2 mph per second?” (Answer: 70 mph.)<br data-start="704" data-end="707">
Point out: you just did a derivative + linear approximation.</p>
<p data-start="769" data-end="800"><strong data-start="769" data-end="798">Core activity (15–20 min)</strong></p>
<ol data-start="801" data-end="1107">
<li data-start="801" data-end="862">
