 logic gates and compositional functions. Think of it as a roadmap you can expand into technical sections.</p>
<hr data-start="251" data-end="254">
<h1 data-start="256" data-end="311">Roadmap: From Logic Gates to Higher-Order Type Theory</h1>
<h2 data-start="313" data-end="347">1. Logic as Primitive Machinery</h2>
<ul data-start="348" data-end="615">
<li data-start="348" data-end="401">
<p data-start="350" data-end="401"><strong data-start="350" data-end="366">Truth values</strong>: encode as voltage levels (0/1).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="402" data-end="477">
<p data-start="404" data-end="477"><strong data-start="404" data-end="419">Logic gates</strong>: AND, OR, NOT → universal basis (NAND or NOR suffices).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="478" data-end="544">
<p data-start="480" data-end="544"><strong data-start="480" data-end="506">Propositional formulas</strong>: finite circuits composed of gates.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="545" data-end="615">
<p data-start="547" data-end="615"><strong data-start="547" data-end="563">Normal forms</strong>: CNF/DNF correspond to canonical circuit layouts.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="617" data-end="707"><em data-start="617" data-end="623">Goal</em>: Show that all of propositional logic is already contained in the algebra of gates.</p>
<hr data-start="709" data-end="712">
<h2 data-start="714" data-end="745">2. Composition and Functions</h2>
<ul data-start="746" data-end="1108">
<li data-start="746" data-end="822">
<p data-start="748" data-end="822"><strong data-start="748" data-end="775">Compositional functions</strong>: outputs of one gate feed inputs of another.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="823" data-end="885">
<p data-start="825" data-end="885"><strong data-start="825" data-end="840">Abstraction</strong>: treat a subcircuit as a “function block.”</p>
</li>
<li data-start="886" data-end="948">
<p data-start="888" data-end="948"><strong data-start="888" data-end="908">Lambda-like step</strong>: label inputs and allow substitution.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="949" data-end="1108">
<p data-start="951" data-end="993"><strong data-start="951" data-end="990">Boolean algebra → λ-calculus bridge</strong>:</p>
<ul data-start="996" data-end="1108">
<li data-start="996" data-end="1037">
<p data-start="998" data-end="1037">Circuits implement Boolean functions.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1040" data-end="1108">
<p data-start="1042" data-end="1108">Currying and uncurrying mimic multi-input/multi-output behavior.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="1110" data-end="1225"><em data-start="1110" data-end="1116">Goal</em>: Derive λ-calculus primitives (application, abstraction, substitution) from compositional function networks.</p>
<hr data-start="1227" data-end="1230">
<h2 data-start="1232" data-end="1258">3. Types as Constraints</h2>
<ul data-start="1259" data-end="1528">
<li data-start="1259" data-end="1317">
<p data-start="1261" data-end="1317"><strong data-start="1261" data-end="1282">Typing discipline</strong>: wire types = allowable signals.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1318" data-end="1377">
<p data-start="1320" data-end="1377"><strong data-start="1320" data-end="1336">Simple types</strong>: propositions as types (Curry–Howard).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1378" data-end="1459">
<p data-start="1380" data-end="1459"><strong data-start="1380" data-end="1399">Dependent types</strong>: circuits parameterized by values (families of circuits).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1460" data-end="1528">
<p data-start="1462" data-end="1528"><strong data-start="1462" data-end="1494">Type checking = verification</strong>: prevents invalid compositions.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="1530" data-end="1622"><em data-start="1530" data-end="1536">Goal</em>: Show how typing emerges naturally once we want circuits/functions to compose safely.</p>
<hr data-start="1624" data-end="1627">
<h2 data-start="1629" data-end="1666">4. Calculus of Constructions (CoC)</h2>
<ul data-start="1667" data-end="2014">
<li data-start="1667" data-end="1719">
<p data-start="1669" data-end="1719"><strong data-start="1669" data-end="1682">Base idea</strong>: unify λ-calculus and type theory.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1720" data-end="1859">
<p data-start="1722" data-end="1747"><strong data-start="1722" data-end="1744">Constructive rules</strong>:</p>
<ul data-start="1750" data-end="1859">
<li data-start="1750" data-end="1775">
<p data-start="1752" data-end="1775">Functions are proofs.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1778" data-end="1807">
<p data-start="1780" data-end="1807">Application is proof use.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1810" data-end="1859">
<p data-start="1812" data-end="1859">Types depend on terms, terms depend on types.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start="1860" data-end="1946">
<p data-start="1862" data-end="1946"><strong data-start="1862" data-end="1885">Inductive structure</strong>: build natural numbers, booleans, lists, trees from rules.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1947" data-end="2014">
<p data-start="1949" data-end="2014"><strong data-start="1949" data-end="1966">Proof objects</strong>: circuits double as evidence of propositions.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="2016" data-end="2112"><em data-start="2016" data-end="2022">Goal</em>: Demonstrate how gate-compositions, once enriched with dependent typing, instantiate CoC.</p>
<hr data-start="2114" data-end="2117">
<h2 data-start="2119" data-end="2156">5. Higher-Order Type Theory (HoTT)</h2>
<ul data-start="2157" data-end="2470">
<li data-start="2157" data-end="2220">
<p data-start="2159" data-end="2220"><strong data-start="2159" data-end="2183">Homotopy perspective</strong>: types as spaces, proofs as paths.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2221" data-end="2314">
<p data-start="2223" data-end="2314"><strong data-start="2223" data-end="2239">Equivalences</strong>: different circuits/proofs that compute the same result are “homotopic.”</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2315" data-end="2371">
<p data-start="2317" data-end="2371"><strong data-start="2317" data-end="2337">Univalence axiom</strong>: isomorphic circuits are equal.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2372" data-end="2470">
<p data-start="2374" data-end="2470"><strong data-start="2374" data-end="2390">Higher paths</strong>: rewrites of rewrites correspond to higher-order transformations of circuits.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="2472" data-end="2579"><em data-start="2472" data-end="2478">Goal</em>: Generalize beyond CoC so that identity, equivalence, and symmetry of constructions are first-class.</p>
<hr data-start="2581" data-end="2584">
<h2 data-start="2586" data-end="2608">6. RSVP Integration</h2>
<ul data-start="2609" data-end="2953">
<li data-start="2609" data-end="2684">
<p data-start="2611" data-end="2684"><strong data-start="2611" data-end="2625">Scalar (Φ)</strong>: capacity = number of possible constructions at a level.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2685" data-end="2750">
<p data-start="2687" data-end="2750"><strong data-start="2687" data-end="2702">Vector (𝒗)</strong>: flow = how functions compose, proofs reduce.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2751" data-end="2840">
<p data-start="2753" data-end="2840"><strong data-start="2753" data-end="2768">Entropy (S)</strong>: disorder = ambiguity, redundancy, error-paths in construction space.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2841" data-end="2953">
<p data-start="2843" data-end="2953"><strong data-start="2843" data-end="2862">Mitigation view</strong>: building CoC/HoTT from gates shrinks entropy of reasoning — enlarges the safe manifold.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="2955" data-end="2958">
<h1 data-start="2960" data-end="2986">Suggested Expansion Path</h1>
<ul data-start="2987" data-end="3260">
<li data-start="2987" data-end="3058">
<p data-start="2989" data-end="3058"><strong data-start="2989" data-end="3000">Stage 1</strong>: Boolean circuits → propositional logic → Curry–Howard.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3059" data-end="3123">
<p data-start="3061" data-end="3123"><strong data-start="3061" data-end="3072">Stage 2</strong>: Circuits + composition → λ-calculus primitives.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3124" data-end="3189">
<p data-start="3126" data-end="3189"><strong data-start="3126" data-end="3137">Stage 3</strong>: Add dependent types → Calculus of Constructions.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3190" data-end="3260">
<p data-start="3192" data-end="3260"><strong data-start="3192" data-end="3203">Stage 4</strong>: Add homotopy equivalences → Higher-Order Type Theory.</p>
</li>
</ul>
