:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="338">Got it — we can indeed make the Python prototype more <strong data-start="54" data-end="80">visual and interactive</strong>, paving the way to 2D/3D visualization. Let me show you how we can extend the working interpreter into a <strong data-start="186" data-end="213">graph-producing backend</strong>. This will let you render SPC terms in any visualization engine (e.g. <code data-start="284" data-end="296">matplotlib</code>, <code data-start="298" data-end="308">networkx</code>, or later <code data-start="319" data-end="330">pythreejs</code>/WebGL).</p>
<p data-start="340" data-end="424">Here’s a minimal extension to the prototype I gave you, adding <strong data-start="403" data-end="423">graph extraction</strong>:</p>
