01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-latex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Historical Antecedents}

The Spherepop Calculus (SPC) integrates several long-standing traditions in logic,
type theory, and categorical semantics.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Lambda Calculus and Type Theory.}
The core syntax of </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\texttt</span></span><span>{Sphere} and </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\texttt</span></span><span>{Pop} traces directly to
Church's untyped </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>-calculus (1930s) and its typed extensions
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{church1940formulation}. The </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Pi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>- and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>-types follow
the Martin–Löf dependent type theory tradition
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{martinlof1975intuitionistic}, later refined into the Calculus of
Constructions (CoC) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{coquand1988calculus} and the Calculus of
Inductive Constructions that underlies Coq.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Categorical Semantics.}
The interpretation of types as objects, terms as morphisms, and
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Pi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>/</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> as exponentials and dependent sums reflects the
Curry–Howard–Lambek correspondence </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{lambek1980categorical}.
The explicit use of a monoidal functor (</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\texttt</span></span><span>{Pop}) connects to
the theory of monoidal categories and coherence theorems
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{maclane1963natural}, while the definition of </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Sphere}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>
as a strict 2-category draws on Street’s axiomatization of 2-categories
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{street1972two}.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Topos Theory.}
The presheaf semantics for SPC situates it within the tradition of
Lawvere’s elementary toposes </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{lawvere1970quantifiers}, which
internalize higher-order intuitionistic logic. The use of subspheres as
propositions and sphere morphisms as proofs follows this tradition.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Probabilistic Semantics.}
The enrichment of SPC with </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\texttt</span></span><span>{Choice} builds on two converging
traditions: probabilistic programming in the style of Kozen’s semantics
of probabilistic programs </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{kozen1981semantics}, and the
distribution monad introduced by Giry </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{giry1982categorical}.
This positions SPC as an extension of the </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>-calculus into
the space of stochastic computation, compatible with modern work on
probabilistic dependent type theory </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{adams2020foundations}.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Parallel and Merge Operators.}
The </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\texttt</span></span><span>{Merge} construct resonates with early work on concurrency
and nondeterminism in process calculi (e.g.</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span>Milner’s CCS
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{milner1980calculus}), but in SPC is reinterpreted categorically as
a tensor product in the monoidal functorial semantics.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Summary.}
Taken together, SPC synthesizes (i) the </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>-calculus and
dependent types, (ii) categorical models via 2-categories and toposes,
and (iii) probabilistic enrichment via distribution monads. In this way
SPC can be read as a new branch in the tradition of constructive
foundations for mathematics and computation, updated for modern
applications in probabilistic reasoning and categorical physics.

</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
<hr data-start="2927" data-end="2930">
<p data-start="2932" data-end="3066">This gives you a narrative arc: <strong data-start="2964" data-end="3065">λ-calculus → dependent types → categorical/topos logic → probabilistic monads → concurrency/merge</strong>.</p>
