The Spherepop Calculus (SPC) draws on and extends several well-established
traditions in logic, type theory, and programming languages.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Functional abstraction.}
Church’s </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>-calculus </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{church1940formulation} provided the original
foundation for higher-order functional computation, and its type-theoretic
extensions underpin modern proof theory and programming languages. SPC inherits
this tradition through </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\texttt</span></span><span>{Sphere} and </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\texttt</span></span><span>{Pop}, which reinterpret
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>-abstraction and application in geometric terms. Dependent type theory
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{martinlof1975intuitionistic} and the Calculus of Constructions
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{coquand1988calculus} supply the logical foundation for SPC’s dependent
extension, allowing </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Pi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>- and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>-types to coexist with concurrency and
probability.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Categorical semantics.}
The Curry–Howard–Lambek correspondence </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{lambek1986categorical} and the
development of categorical logic provide the structural bridge between syntax
and semantics. Mac Lane’s coherence theorems </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{maclane1963natural},
Lawvere’s elementary toposes </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{lawvere1970quantifiers}, and Street’s
axiomatization of monads and 2-categories </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{street1972two} establish the
formal setting in which SPC’s denotational semantics is formulated. In this
framework, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\texttt</span></span><span>{Merge} is modeled as a tensor, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\texttt</span></span><span>{Pop} as evaluation,
and the entire calculus as a presheaf topos enriched with probabilistic
structure.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Probabilistic programming and monads.}
Kozen’s semantics of probabilistic programs </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{kozen1981semantics} and Giry’s
distribution monad </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{giry1982categorical} anchor SPC’s probabilistic
semantics. More recent work in probabilistic type theory
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{adams2020foundations} and categorical probability
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{jacobs2015new,fritz2020synthetic} provides a compositional account of
stochastic computation, which SPC internalizes in the </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\texttt</span></span><span>{Choice} operator.
Contemporary probabilistic programming systems such as Church, Anglican, and
WebPPL </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{goodman2014design,van2018introduction,gordon2014probabilistic}
demonstrate the practical importance of integrating probability into programming
languages; SPC contributes a dependent, categorical foundation that could
support such systems.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Concurrency and process calculi.}
Milner’s CCS and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>-calculus </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{milner1980calculus,milner1999communicating}
define interaction through communication and mobility of names. SPC’s
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\texttt</span></span><span>{Merge} is reminiscent of nondeterministic parallel composition, but its
categorical semantics treats concurrency as a tensor product rather than as
name-passing. This positions SPC closer to categorical models of concurrency,
while still reflecting the operational intuitions of process calculi.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Proof assistants and implementations.}
The Calculus of Inductive Constructions is realized in Coq
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{bertot2004interactive}, while Agda </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{norell2009dependently} embodies a
dependently typed programming language with interactive proof capabilities. SPC
shares their constructive spirit but extends it with native probabilistic and
concurrent constructs. This suggests a path toward future mechanizations of SPC
in proof assistants, combining the rigor of type theory with stochastic and
parallel reasoning.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Summary.}
In summary, SPC positions itself at the intersection of four lines of work:
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>-calculus and dependent types, categorical and topos-theoretic models,
probabilistic semantics and programming, and concurrency theory. Its novelty
lies in synthesizing these strands into a single, geometrically motivated
calculus where abstraction, parallelism, and probability are first-class and
compositional.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
<hr data-start="3886" data-end="3889">
<p data-start="3891" data-end="3961">This ties all the references together into a <strong data-start="3936" data-end="3958">coherent narrative</strong>:</p>
<ul data-start="3962" data-end="4167">
<li data-start="3962" data-end="4015">
<p data-start="3964" data-end="4015">λ-calculus → dependent types → categorical logic.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4016" data-end="4079">
<p data-start="4018" data-end="4079">Probabilistic semantics → modern probabilistic programming.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4080" data-end="4127">
<p data-start="4082" data-end="4127">Concurrency → categorical reinterpretation.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4128" data-end="4167">
<p data-start="4130" data-end="4167">Implementations → Coq/Agda lineage.</p>
</li>
</ul>
