<h3 data-start="1521" data-end="1535">Why these?</h3>
<ul data-start="1537" data-end="2079">
<li data-start="1537" data-end="1598">
<p data-start="1539" data-end="1598"><strong data-start="1539" data-end="1561">Barendregt (1984):</strong> canonical reference on λ-calculus.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1599" data-end="1708">
<p data-start="1601" data-end="1708"><strong data-start="1601" data-end="1619">Girard (1987):</strong> linear logic — relevant if you want to situate SPC against resource-sensitive calculi.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1709" data-end="1802">
<p data-start="1711" data-end="1802"><strong data-start="1711" data-end="1728">Moggi (1991):</strong> monads as a notion of computation — bridges SPC to distribution monads.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1803" data-end="1894">
<p data-start="1805" data-end="1894"><strong data-start="1805" data-end="1823">Pierce (2002):</strong> standard PL textbook, good for readers outside the λ-calculus niche.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1895" data-end="2002">
<p data-start="1897" data-end="2002"><strong data-start="1897" data-end="1931">Milner, Parrow, Walker (1992):</strong> π-calculus “part I/II” definitive paper, complements Milner’s books.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2003" data-end="2079">
<p data-start="2005" data-end="2079"><strong data-start="2005" data-end="2023">Awodey (2010):</strong> a modern, widely cited reference for category theory.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="2081" data-end="2084">
<p data-start="2086" data-end="2528">👉 If you add these to your <code data-start="2114" data-end="2120">.bib</code>, your Related Work and Positioning sections will feel fully grounded: from <strong data-start="2196" data-end="2218">λ-calculus history</strong> (Church, Barendregt) → <strong data-start="2242" data-end="2251">types</strong> (Martin-Löf, Coquand &amp; Huet, Pierce) → <strong data-start="2291" data-end="2316">categorical semantics</strong> (Lambek, Mac Lane, Lawvere, Awodey) → <strong data-start="2355" data-end="2379">monads &amp; probability</strong> (Moggi, Giry, Kozen, Jacobs, Fritz) → <strong data-start="2418" data-end="2433">concurrency</strong> (Milner, π-calculus) → <strong data-start="2457" data-end="2493">modern probabilistic programming</strong> (Goodman, Gordon, van de Meent).</p>
