se dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="161">I think what you’re seeing is not <em data-start="34" data-end="53">bleeding diagrams</em> so much as <em data-start="65" data-end="96">LaTeX’s default page geometry</em>. By default, the <code data-start="114" data-end="123">article</code> class with no extra options produces:</p>
<ul data-start="163" data-end="451">
<li data-start="163" data-end="271">
<p data-start="165" data-end="271"><strong data-start="165" data-end="193">1 inch (2.54 cm) margins</strong> on all sides (with <code data-start="213" data-end="236">\geometry{margin=1in}</code> you’ve explicitly set this too).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="272" data-end="351">
<p data-start="274" data-end="351">A relatively narrow text block (≈ 6.5 in wide × 9 in tall on letter paper).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="352" data-end="451">
<p data-start="354" data-end="451">So even though the paper is A4 or US Letter (8.5×11 in), only a 6.5×9 rectangle is used for text.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="453" data-end="611">That’s why your diagrams <em data-start="478" data-end="484">feel</em> like they’re “pushing into the margin” — in fact they’re just wider than the 6.5 in text block, so TeX reports overfull boxes.</p>
<h3 data-start="613" data-end="649">How to make the text block wider</h3>
<p data-start="650" data-end="717">If you want to reclaim some space, you have a few standard options:</p>
<ol data-start="719" data-end="1560">
<li data-start="719" data-end="923">
