ty:hidden first:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="181">Good move switching to <code data-start="23" data-end="33">tabularx</code>. The issue is that right now you’ve set <em data-start="74" data-end="88">every column</em> as <code data-start="92" data-end="95">X</code>, which makes LaTeX try to equalize their widths — that’s why things still overflow.</p>
<p data-start="183" data-end="202">What you want is:</p>
<ul data-start="204" data-end="379">
<li data-start="204" data-end="258">
<p data-start="206" data-end="258"><strong data-start="206" data-end="234">first column (<code data-start="222" data-end="231">Feature</code>)</strong>: fixed width, say <code data-start="253" data-end="256">l</code></p>
</li>
<li data-start="259" data-end="318">
<p data-start="261" data-end="318"><strong data-start="261" data-end="279">middle columns</strong>: flexible <code data-start="290" data-end="293">X</code>, so they shrink/expand</p>
</li>
<li data-start="319" data-end="379">
<p data-start="321" data-end="379"><strong data-start="321" data-end="335">math cells</strong>: wrap in <code data-start="345" data-end="351">\(\)</code> or <code data-start="355" data-end="362">$...$</code> but keep short</p>
</li>
</ul>
